# Aptus AI Coding Rules

## Scope
- Make the smallest change that satisfies the requested task.
- Do not rewrite unrelated files.
- Do not add a frontend framework, ORM, or dependency without asking first.
- Prefer semantic HTML, modern CSS, Node standard library, and small ES modules.

## Security
- Never request, print, store, or log secrets, tokens, cookies, passwords, API keys, or real user data.
- Treat all request data as untrusted. Validate inputs server-side with allowlists and length limits.
- Escape untrusted values when rendering HTML. Use parameterized database queries only.
- Enforce authorization server-side for every protected resource.
- Do not use localStorage for auth tokens.
- Do not weaken CORS, CSP, cookie, TLS, or CSRF protections.
- Do not invent cryptography or authentication flows.

## Privacy
- Use illustrative data only in tests and examples.
- Do not add analytics that records education, DOB, category, gender, disability, search text, or eligibility decisions without explicit approval.
- Do not include sensitive values in URLs, logs, client errors, or telemetry.

## Quality
- Follow `docs/CODING_STANDARD.md` for naming, focused modules, sparse comments,
  skill/version checks, review ownership and context-reset continuity.
- Add or update tests for behaviour changes.
- Preserve no-JavaScript baseline flows where feasible.
- Use accessible labels, keyboard operation, focus behaviour, and meaningful error messages.
- Explain every new dependency and its maintenance/security rationale.
- Return a compact plan, diff summary, test results, and remaining risks.

## Aptus change checklist

### Scope
- [ ] The change is small and matches the approved task.
- [ ] No unrelated refactor or generated files are included.
- [ ] No new dependency was added without a documented reason.

### Authentication and access
- [ ] Protected routes require a valid server-verified session.
- [ ] Object ownership is checked on the server.
- [ ] Logout invalidates the server session.
- [ ] Session cookies are Secure, HttpOnly, and appropriately SameSite.
- [ ] State-changing cookie-authenticated requests have CSRF protection.

### Input and output
- [ ] All request values are validated server-side.
- [ ] HTML output escapes untrusted content.
- [ ] Database queries are parameterized.
- [ ] Error responses do not expose stack traces, secrets, or internal paths.

### Privacy
- [ ] No real user data, credentials, tokens, or secrets were used in tests or AI prompts.
- [ ] No sensitive applicant fields were added to URLs, logs, analytics, or browser storage.
- [ ] Data collection has a documented product purpose.

### Quality
- [ ] Unit/integration tests pass.
- [ ] Critical browser flow passes.
- [ ] Keyboard and no-JavaScript baseline have been checked.
- [ ] Performance impact has been considered.
